      "text": "We start with the given equation of the circle:\n\\[x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0.\\]\nFirst, we complete the square for the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nFor the \\(x\\) terms, \\(x^2 - 6x\\), we take half of \\(-6\\), which is \\(-3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\). So, we add and subtract \\(9\\):\n\\[x^2 - 6x = (x - 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\) terms, \\(y^2 + 2y\\), we take half of \\(2\\), which is \\(1\\), and then square it to get \\(1\\). So, we add and subtract \\(1\\):\n\\[y^2 + 2y = (y + 1)^2 - 1.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the original equation, we get:\n\\[(x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0.\\]\nSimplifying the constants on the left side, we have:\n\\[(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 4 = 0.\\]\nAdding \\(4\\) to both sides of the equation, we obtain:\n\\[(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4.\\]\nThis is the standard form of the equation of a circle \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius. By comparing, we see that the center of the circle is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
